/**
@page vad Voice Activity Detection (VAD)

sherpa-onnx supports three VAD model families through the
@ref SherpaOnnxCreateVoiceActivityDetector() API. Configure exactly one
by filling in the corresponding field of @ref SherpaOnnxVadModelConfig.

@see SherpaOnnxCreateVoiceActivityDetector, SherpaOnnxVadModelConfig

@tableofcontents

@section vad_silero Silero VAD

Silero VAD is a widely used voice activity detection model.
The recommended window size is 512 samples.

@code
SherpaOnnxVadModelConfig config;
memset(&config, 0, sizeof(config));
config.silero_vad.model = "./silero_vad.onnx";
config.silero_vad.threshold = 0.25f;
config.silero_vad.min_silence_duration = 0.5f;
config.silero_vad.min_speech_duration = 0.5f;
config.silero_vad.max_speech_duration = 10.0f;
config.silero_vad.window_size = 512;
config.sample_rate = 16000;
config.num_threads = 1;

const SherpaOnnxVoiceActivityDetector *vad =
    SherpaOnnxCreateVoiceActivityDetector(&config, 30.0f);
@endcode

Model file: `silero_vad.onnx`

Example source: [vad-whisper-c-api.c](https://github.com/k2-fsa/sherpa-onnx/blob/master/c-api-examples/vad-whisper-c-api.c)

@section vad_ten Ten VAD

Ten VAD is an alternative VAD model. The recommended window size is 256
samples.

@code
SherpaOnnxVadModelConfig config;
memset(&config, 0, sizeof(config));
config.ten_vad.model = "./ten-vad.onnx";
config.ten_vad.threshold = 0.25f;
config.ten_vad.min_silence_duration = 0.5f;
config.ten_vad.min_speech_duration = 0.5f;
config.ten_vad.max_speech_duration = 10.0f;
config.ten_vad.window_size = 256;
config.sample_rate = 16000;
config.num_threads = 1;

const SherpaOnnxVoiceActivityDetector *vad =
    SherpaOnnxCreateVoiceActivityDetector(&config, 30.0f);
@endcode

Model file: `ten-vad.onnx`

Example source: [vad-whisper-c-api.c](https://github.com/k2-fsa/sherpa-onnx/blob/master/c-api-examples/vad-whisper-c-api.c)

@section vad_nomo_pvad NOMO-PVAD (target-speaker VAD)

Silero VAD and Ten VAD answer "is anybody talking?". NOMO-PVAD answers "is the
*enrolled* speaker talking?", so it needs a d-vector of the target speaker in
addition to the model. Use the 3d-speaker ERes2NetV2 model to compute it, since
that is the model NOMO-PVAD was trained with.

@code
// Compute the d-vector of the target speaker once.
SherpaOnnxSpeakerEmbeddingExtractorConfig ex_config;
memset(&ex_config, 0, sizeof(ex_config));
ex_config.model =
    "./3dspeaker_speech_eres2netv2_sv_zh-cn_16k-common.onnx";

const SherpaOnnxSpeakerEmbeddingExtractor *ex =
    SherpaOnnxCreateSpeakerEmbeddingExtractor(&ex_config);

// const float *target = ... see speaker-identification-c-api.c

SherpaOnnxVadModelConfig config;
memset(&config, 0, sizeof(config));
config.nomo_pvad.model = "./nomo-pvad.onnx";
config.nomo_pvad.speaker_embedding = target;
config.nomo_pvad.speaker_embedding_dim =
    SherpaOnnxSpeakerEmbeddingExtractorDim(ex);
config.nomo_pvad.threshold = 0.7f;
config.nomo_pvad.min_silence_duration = 0.5f;
config.nomo_pvad.min_speech_duration = 0.25f;
config.nomo_pvad.max_speech_duration = 20.0f;
config.sample_rate = 16000;
config.num_threads = 1;

const SherpaOnnxVoiceActivityDetector *vad =
    SherpaOnnxCreateVoiceActivityDetector(&config, 30.0f);
@endcode

Model file: `nomo-pvad.onnx`, plus a 3d-speaker ERes2NetV2 model for enrollment

Example source: [target-speaker-vad-c-api.c](https://github.com/k2-fsa/sherpa-onnx/blob/master/c-api-examples/target-speaker-vad-c-api.c)

@see SherpaOnnxNomoPvadModelConfig
*/
